<BaseLayout title="Pulsar Timing Array Research — Jonas El Gammal" description="PTA non-Gaussianity, whitening, and accelerated Bayesian inference with JAX.">
  <div class="shell"><header class="page-head"><span class="eyebrow">Research · Pulsar Timing Arrays</span><h1>Signals, statistics,<br/>and what survives.</h1><p>I work on both the statistical limits and the computational bottlenecks of nanohertz gravitational-wave inference.</p></header></div>
  <article class="prose">
    <span class="status">Latest paper · under review</span><h2>Can PTAs detect gravitational-wave non-Gaussianity?</h2><p>Our latest work asks whether model-agnostic statistics can distinguish an astrophysical population from a cosmological gravitational-wave background. The answer is largely negative—but the reason is scientifically useful.</p><p><strong>Chiara Cecchini, Jonas El Gammal, Gabriele Franciolini, and Mauro Pieroni.</strong> <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.05157">arXiv:2605.05157v2</a>.</p>
    <h3>Whitening is essential</h3><p>The Hellings–Downs response correlates PTA data. Applying distributional tests before decorrelation creates false positives even under an isotropic Gaussian null.</p>
    <figure class="figure"><img src="/assets/img/pta_ng_whitening_v2.svg" alt="Whitening calibration tests for the PTA non-Gaussianity analysis"/><figcaption>Without whitening, nominal p-values are miscalibrated. Whitening restores the expected null behaviour. Vector figure from the latest arXiv version. <a href="/assets/img/pta_ng_whitening_v2.pdf">Open PDF</a>.</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Why non-Gaussian structure disappears</h3><p>After the overall amplitude is estimated from the same realization, variance differences are removed. The broad pulsar antenna patterns then average the sky strongly enough to drive intrinsically different source distributions toward the same Gaussian null.</p>
    <figure class="figure figure--portrait"><img src="/assets/img/pta_ng_scale_v2.svg" alt="Central-limit and scale-estimation results for PTA source populations"/><figcaption>Scale estimation and the broad PTA response wash out most model-agnostic non-Gaussian information. Vector figure from the latest arXiv version. <a href="/assets/img/pta_ng_scale_v2.pdf">Open PDF</a>.</figcaption></figure>
    <h2>Fast PTA inference</h2><p>Separately, I develop <a href="https://github.com/jonaselgammal/discoverysamplers"><strong>discoverysamplers</strong></a>, a clean interface between the JAX-enabled Discovery PTA likelihood and inference backends including Eryn, nessai, JAX-NS, and GPry.</p>
    <div class="sampler-flow" aria-label="discoverysamplers architecture"><strong>Discovery model</strong><span>shared priors + adapter</span><div><b>Eryn</b><b>nessai</b><b>JAX-NS</b><b>GPry</b></div></div>
  </article>
